      "DEFY built its reputation on nano-particle UV technology in its tinted stains, and the Crystal Clear sealer borrows the same defensive chemistry without adding a single pigment particle. That distinction matters more than it sounds. Most clear sealers offer essentially zero UV defense, which is fine if you've accepted that PT lumber will silver over a year or two — but DEFY's additive measurably slowed that color shift in our test without shifting the wood's own tone toward amber or yellow the way some 'UV-protected clear' products do. We used this specifically on a deck built from standard wet-treated ACQ lumber that had just cleared our water-bead dryness test at the seven-month mark, still reading slightly elevated on a moisture meter. DEFY penetrated evenly where a thicker-bodied competitor sealer beaded and sat on the surface of the same boards in a side-by-side test strip. The zinc-based mildewcide earned its keep on the shaded north side of the deck, where a control section treated with a mildewcide-free sealer developed faint black spotting by month nine while the DEFY section stayed clean. Application requires a bit more patience than Thompson's — rough-sawn PT lumber drinks the first coat almost immediately and needs a second thin coat about an hour later to build real water resistance, which adds time on a large deck. The finish itself is a genuine flat clear with none of the slightly plasticky sheen some water-based sealers leave behind. For anyone sealing PT lumber close to the early edge of the recommended drying window, this is the product with the most margin for error.",
