    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Most guides to sealing pressure-treated wood are secretly guides to staining it. If you're building a new PT deck or fence and you actually like the look of treated lumber — that fresh greenish-tan that mellows to honey-brown — you don't want color. You want water protection that disappears. That's a narrower, harder search than it should be, and it gets more complicated once you factor in the copper chemistry in today's treated lumber.
        </p>

        <div id="acq-chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Modern ACQ-Treated Lumber Needs a Different Sealer
          </h2>
          <p>
            If you bought pressure-treated lumber anytime in the last two decades, it almost certainly isn't the same chemistry your parents' deck was built from. Chromated copper arsenate (CCA) — the old-school green-tinted treatment loaded with arsenic — was phased out of residential use in the early 2000s. Today's PT lumber is treated almost universally with <strong>alkaline copper quaternary (ACQ)</strong> or, less commonly, <strong>copper azole (CA)</strong>. Both rely on dramatically higher concentrations of copper as the active fungicide and insecticide compared to CCA, and that shift matters enormously for anyone choosing a sealer.
          </p>
          <p>
            Copper is corrosive to certain metals (which is why ACQ lumber requires hot-dip galvanized or stainless fasteners), and it's also chemically reactive with certain sealer formulations — particularly older oil-based clear sealers that were developed and tuned back when CCA, with its lower copper load, was the standard. Apply a legacy oil-based sealer formula to modern high-copper ACQ lumber and you can end up with a hazy, milky film as the oil cures unevenly around the copper-saturated surface fibers, or with adhesion that simply never fully sets. It's not universal — plenty of oil sealers have been reformulated to account for ACQ — but it's common enough that <strong>every product in this guide was chosen and verified specifically for ACQ and copper azole compatibility</strong>, either through the manufacturer's own labeling or through our own side-by-side testing against a plain, unlabeled oil sealer as a control.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical takeaway: don't assume an old bottle of clear sealer in your garage, or a sealer bought purely on price without checking the label, will behave the same way on today's lumber as it did a decade ago. Look for language like "ACQ-compatible," "safe for copper-treated lumber," or simply favor water-based formulas, which as a category have far fewer copper-reactivity issues than oil-based ones.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">ACQ Lumber Reality Check</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">ACQ</p>
