        <div id="wait-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 6-Month to 1-Year Rule: When New PT Lumber Is Ready to Seal
          </h2>
          <p>
            Pressure treatment forces preservative deep into lumber under high pressure, and the process leaves standard wet-treated boards genuinely saturated — sometimes carrying moisture content well above 75% right off the truck. Seal over that and the sealer can't penetrate; it sits on the surface, cures unevenly, and fails within a season. This is the single most common mistake we see with brand-new PT decks and fences, and it's the reason the old "wait 6 months to a year" rule of thumb for new PT lumber exists.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Not All PT Lumber Dries at the Same Rate</h3>
          <p>
            The 6-months-to-a-year window is a conservative average for standard wet-treated lumber, and your actual timeline depends heavily on three factors: climate, board thickness, and whether the lumber is kiln-dried after treatment (KDAT). <strong>KDAT lumber</strong>, which is run through a kiln after the treatment process specifically to drive out residual moisture, can often be sealed in as little as 30 to 60 days — check for a KDAT stamp on the board ends. Standard wet-treated lumber in hot, dry climates might be ready in three to four months; in humid climates like the Southeast or Pacific Northwest, a full year isn't unreasonable, especially for thick 4x4 and 6x6 posts that hold moisture at their core long after the surface feels dry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Test the Wood — Don't Just Trust the Calendar</h3>
          <p>
            The most reliable way to know if PT lumber is ready is to test it directly rather than count months. Three methods, in order of simplicity:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Water bead test:</strong> Sprinkle a tablespoon of water onto the surface. If it beads and sits for more than a few minutes, the wood is still too wet. If it soaks in within a couple of minutes, the wood is ready to accept sealer.</li>
            <li><strong>Moisture meter test:</strong> A pin-type moisture meter is the most objective method. Most sealer manufacturers recommend a reading below roughly 15-19% before application; test in several spots, including the shaded underside of deck boards, which dries slower than the sun-facing top.</li>
            <li><strong>Color test:</strong> Fresh, wet PT lumber has a distinctly greenish-tan hue and often feels slightly damp or cool to the touch even in dry weather. As it dries, it lightens toward a honey-tan. A board that still looks green and feels heavy for its size almost certainly hasn't finished drying.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you're impatient to protect a brand-new deck immediately, know that the wood's own preservative treatment is already doing the job of preventing rot and insect damage during that waiting period — a sealer at this stage is purely about cosmetic waterproofing (preventing cupping, splitting, and surface checking from moisture cycling), not structural protection. It's genuinely fine to wait.
          </p>
        </div>

