        <div id="how-to-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Seal Pressure-Treated Wood: Step by Step
          </h2>
          <p>
            Sealing treated lumber is simpler than staining it — there's no color to get even, no lap marks to worry about matching — but the sequence still matters, especially on new ACQ wood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: Confirm the Wood Is Actually Dry</h3>
          <p>
            Before anything else, run the water bead test or check with a moisture meter as described above. Sealing PT lumber before it's dry enough is the single biggest cause of sealer failure, and no product in this guide — or anywhere else — can penetrate wood that's still saturated from the treatment process.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Clean the Surface</h3>
          <p>
            Even new PT lumber picks up sawdust, mill residue, and handling grime. Wash with a dedicated deck cleaner or a mild oxygen-bleach solution and a stiff brush, then rinse thoroughly. Keep pressure washers under about 1,200-1,500 PSI — higher pressure raises the grain and creates an uneven surface that absorbs sealer inconsistently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Let It Dry Fully Again</h3>
          <p>
            After washing, give the wood at least 48 hours of dry weather before sealing — longer in humid climates. Sealing over surface moisture from cleaning, separate from the internal treatment moisture discussed above, causes the same kind of uneven absorption and cloudy finish.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 4: Apply in Mild, Overcast Conditions</h3>
          <p>
            Seal at 50-85°F, ideally on an overcast day or in the shade. Direct sun causes water-based sealers especially to flash-dry on the surface before they penetrate, which is exactly the surface-film problem you're trying to avoid on copper-treated wood. Apply with a sprayer, roller, or pad in manageable sections, working with the grain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 5: Watch for Reaction Signs on the First Board</h3>
          <p>
            Always test a small, inconspicuous section first — a hidden joist, a fence end post — before committing to the whole project. If you see any clouding, haze, or the sealer failing to soak in evenly within a few minutes, stop and switch products rather than sealing the whole structure with something that's reacting poorly to the copper in your specific lumber batch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 6: Cure Before Traffic or Rain</h3>
          <p>
            Keep foot traffic off for 24 hours minimum and check the forecast for at least a day of dry weather after application. Most clear sealers reach full water repellency within a few days as the remaining carrier fully evaporates or cures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Surface Prep Before Sealing Treated Lumber
          </h2>
          <p>
            Prep for a clear sealer is lighter than prep for a stain — there's no old color to strip and no mill glaze concern the way there is with cedar — but the two ACQ-specific traps below account for most of the sealer failures we've seen on treated wood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Don't Skip the Compatibility Test</h3>
          <p>
            Because copper content varies by lumber supplier and even by batch, a sealer that worked perfectly on one deck can occasionally behave differently on another. Always test a hidden section first, especially with any oil-based sealer, before committing the whole project.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Check Every Board, Not Just the Deck Surface</h3>
          <p>
            Ground-contact posts, joists, and the undersides of deck boards often dry much slower than the sun-exposed top surface. If you're sealing a whole structure, test moisture content at several points, including framing members, not just the walking surface — a deck floor can read dry while the joists beneath it are still holding treatment moisture.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistakes That Ruin a Sealer Job on PT Lumber</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-red-900 text-sm">
              <li>• Sealing new PT lumber before it's dry (stain sits on top and fails within months)</li>
              <li>• Using an older, unlabeled oil-based clear sealer that reacts with ACQ's copper content</li>
              <li>• Applying in direct sun, causing flash-drying before the sealer can penetrate</li>
              <li>• Pressure washing above 1,500 PSI and furring the surface grain</li>
              <li>• Skipping the small test-patch application on new lumber batches</li>
              <li>• Assuming a clear sealer will stop graying — only pigment does that</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

