        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best clear sealer for pressure-treated wood?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Thompson's WaterSeal Advanced Natural Wood Protector is our top overall pick for clear sealing pressure-treated lumber — it's formulated to bond with modern ACQ chemistry, stays genuinely colorless, and costs little enough to reapply on schedule. DEFY Crystal Clear is the best choice for newer PT lumber that has only just cleared the recommended drying window, and Seasonite Green Defender is the strongest option for high-retention, ground-contact ACQ lumber where adhesion problems are most common.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I wait to seal new pressure-treated wood?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Standard wet-treated PT lumber typically needs 6 months to a year to dry enough for a sealer to properly penetrate, though kiln-dried-after-treatment (KDAT) lumber can often be sealed within 30-60 days. Don't go by the calendar alone — perform the water bead test: sprinkle water on the surface, and if it absorbs within a few minutes rather than beading on top, the wood is dry enough. A pin-type moisture meter reading below about 15-19% is a more precise confirmation.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my clear sealer look cloudy or hazy on pressure-treated wood?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Cloudiness or a milky film on freshly sealed PT lumber is usually a chemical reaction between an older oil-based sealer formula and the elevated copper content in modern ACQ (alkaline copper quaternary) or copper azole treatments. Older sealer chemistries weren't designed around today's copper-heavy preservatives. The fix is switching to a water-based sealer or an oil-based product specifically reformulated and labeled as compatible with ACQ lumber, such as the products in this guide.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a clear sealer stop pressure-treated wood from turning gray?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A fully clear sealer offers little to no UV protection, since UV resistance comes from pigment, and clear sealers by definition carry none. PT lumber sealed with a clear product will still gradually gray from sun exposure, though it will resist cupping, splitting, and warping from moisture. A semi-clear sealer with a small amount of transparent iron-oxide pigment, like Rain Guard Woodland Premium, slows the graying noticeably while still looking essentially unstained.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a clear sealer or a tinted stain better for pressure-treated wood?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">It depends on whether you want to keep PT lumber's natural greenish-tan color visible. A clear or semi-clear sealer waterproofs the wood without changing its appearance, which is the right choice if you like the look of raw treated lumber or want it to weather naturally. A tinted stain adds color and generally better UV protection, which is worth it if you want to match other wood on the property or actively prevent graying. Our companion guide on the best deck stain for pressure-treated wood covers the tinted-color option in depth.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a special sealer for ACQ-treated lumber?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">You don't necessarily need a niche specialty product, but you do need a sealer verified to be compatible with ACQ (alkaline copper quaternary) chemistry, which has largely replaced older CCA treatments. Look for products that specifically mention ACQ or copper azole compatibility on the label. Water-based sealers are generally safer bets than legacy oil-based formulas, several of which were developed before ACQ became the industry standard and can react with its higher copper content.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
